<header class=3D"hero">
  <div class=3D"crumbs">System Design Interviews <span>=E2=80=BA</span> Air=
line &amp; Travel <span>=E2=80=BA</span> Delta</div>
  <h1>Design an Airline Booking System</h1>
  <p class=3D"sub">Search nonstop flights, lock seats for 15 minutes, take =
payment exactly once, and issue e-tickets =E2=80=94 without ever selling a =
seat twice.</p>
  <div class=3D"meta"><span>=E2=8F=B1 45 min read</span><span>=F0=9F=94=8C =
6 APIs</span><span>=F0=9F=97=84 11 tables</span><span>=F0=9F=A7=AD 8 diagra=
ms</span><span>=F0=9F=94=8D 13 deep dives</span></div>
  <div class=3D"intro">
    <h4>What are we building?</h4>
    <p>The system behind delta.com and the Fly Delta app (and the partner A=
PIs used by travel agents): a customer searches a route and date, picks a f=
are and maybe a seat, pays, and gets a ticket. Search is a <strong>volume</=
strong> problem =E2=80=94 about 1,000 searches per booking. Booking is a <s=
trong>correctness</strong> problem =E2=80=94 a seat can't be sold twice and=
 a card can't be charged twice.</p>
    <div class=3D"chips"><span>One-way</span><span>Nonstop only</span><span=
>1 flight per booking</span><span>=E2=89=A4 9 passengers</span><span>No ove=
rbooking</span><span>15-minute holds</span><span>No sharding</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=3D"hard">
    <div><b>=F0=9F=94=92 Seat locking</b><p>Two people click 12A at the sam=
e millisecond. One conditional UPDATE decides.</p></div>
    <div><b>=E2=8F=B3 Holds that expire</b><p>Seats are held during checkou=
t and released automatically =E2=80=94 but never mid-payment.</p></div>
    <div><b>=F0=9F=92=B3 Money exactly once</b><p>Idempotency keys, authori=
ze =E2=86=92 commit =E2=86=92 capture, and a reconciler for crashes.</p></d=
iv>
  </div>
</header>
<section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"-questions-to-ask-the-interviewer"><span c=
lass=3D"sec-n">1.</span> Clarifying Questions</h2><p class=3D"ask">=E2=80=
=9CQuestions to Ask the Interviewer=E2=80=9D</p><div class=3D"cards qgrid">=
<p class=3D"lead">Pin down scope, what "a seat" means, how long we may hold=
 one, and who else sells our inventory, before drawing anything.</p><div cl=
ass=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"1-which-user-flows-are-in-scope-search-booking-seat-=
selection-payment-manage-booking-check-in"><span class=3D"qn">Q1</span>Whic=
h user flows are in scope? Search, booking, seat selection, payment, manage=
 booking, check-in?</h3><div class=3D"qa why"><span class=3D"qa-l">Why ask<=
/span><p>Each flow is a different service with a different consistency need=
. Search is read-heavy and can be slightly stale. Booking and seat selectio=
n must be strongly consistent. Check-in and boarding bring in airport syste=
ms, which can blow up the scope.</p></div><div class=3D"qa ans"><span class=
=3D"qa-l">Interviewer answer</span><p>Search flights, pick a fare, pick sea=
ts, pay, get an e-ticket, and view, change or cancel a booking. Check-in, b=
aggage, crew and loyalty accrual are out of scope.</p></div></div><div clas=
s=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"2-who-sells-our-seats-only-deltacom-and-the-app-or-als=
o-travel-agents-and-otas"><span class=3D"qn">Q2</span>Who sells our seats? =
Only delta.com and the app, or also travel agents and OTAs?</h3><div class=
=3D"qa why"><span class=3D"qa-l">Why ask</span><p>Airlines sell the same se=
ats through many channels: their own site and app, GDSs (Sabre, Amadeus, Tr=
avelport) used by travel agents, OTAs (Expedia) and the airport / call cent=
er. If every channel reads and writes one inventory, we need a single sourc=
e of truth and per-partner rate limits. <span class=3D"pill our">our additi=
on</span></p></div><div class=3D"qa ans"><span class=3D"qa-l">Interviewer a=
nswer</span><p>Our system is the single source of truth for inventory. Dire=
ct channels call our APIs; GDS and OTA partners call the same APIs through =
a partner gateway. Focus on direct channels.</p></div></div><div class=3D"c=
ard"><h3 id=3D"3-what-is-the-expected-scale"><span class=3D"qn">Q3</span>Wh=
at is the expected scale?</h3><div class=3D"qa why"><span class=3D"qa-l">Wh=
y ask</span><p>Sizes the search cache and the database, tells us whether on=
e database is enough, and how hot a single flight can get.</p></div><div cl=
ass=3D"qa ans"><span class=3D"qa-l">Interviewer answer</span><p>About 5,000=
 flights a day and about 550K passengers a day (~200M a year). Flights are =
on sale about 11 months (~331 days) ahead. Searches are roughly 1,000=C3=97=
 bookings once partners and metasearch (Google Flights, Kayak) are counted.=
 Fare sales cause 10=C3=97 spikes.</p></div></div><div class=3D"card"><h3 i=
d=3D"4-do-users-pick-a-specific-seat-12a-or-only-a-fare"><span class=3D"qn"=
>Q4</span>Do users pick a specific seat (12A), or only a fare?</h3><div cla=
ss=3D"qa why"><span class=3D"qa-l">Why ask</span><p>This is the airline ver=
sion of the movie-booking seat question, but with a twist: an airline sells=
 <strong>two different things</strong>. <span class=3D"pill our">our additi=
on</span></p></div><ul class=3D"bl"><li><div class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>A pl=
ace on the flight in a fare class</strong> (e.g. Main Cabin, class K at $18=
9). This is a count: "how many K seats are left?"</p></div></li><li><div cl=
ass=3D"li-b"><p><strong>A specific seat on the seat map</strong> (12A). Thi=
s is optional at booking time; passengers who don't pick one get one at che=
ck-in.</p></div></li></ul><div class=3D"qa ans"><span class=3D"qa-l">Interv=
iewer answer</span><p>Both. Every booking takes a place in a fare class. Se=
at selection is optional (and blocked by some fare rules, e.g. basic econom=
y), but a chosen seat must never be given to two people.</p></div></div><di=
v class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"5-do-we-allow-overbooking"><span class=3D"qn">Q5=
</span>Do we allow overbooking?</h3><div class=3D"qa why"><span class=3D"qa=
-l">Why ask</span><p>Real airlines often sell more tickets than physical se=
ats because some passengers don't show up. If that's allowed, the "never ov=
ersell" rule changes to "never sell above a Revenue Management limit". If n=
ot, physical seats are a hard cap. <span class=3D"pill our">our addition</s=
pan></p></div><div class=3D"qa ans"><span class=3D"qa-l">Interviewer answer=
</span><p>No. Never sell more places in a cabin than it has physical seats.=
 Every ticketed passenger is guaranteed a seat.</p></div></div><div class=
=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"6-how-long-can-a-user-hold-seats-while-paying"><span cl=
ass=3D"qn">Q6</span>How long can a user hold seats while paying?</h3><div c=
lass=3D"qa why"><span class=3D"qa-l">Why ask</span><p>Decides the hold TTL,=
 the expiry mechanism, and the race between "hold expired" and "payment in =
progress". Too long and bots hoard seats; too short and real users lose sea=
ts while typing card details.</p></div><div class=3D"qa ans"><span class=3D=
"qa-l">Interviewer answer</span><p>15 minutes from when the user starts the=
 booking. If they don't pay in time, the seats go back on sale.</p></div></=
div><div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"7-do-we-own-pricing"><span class=3D"qn">Q=
7</span>Do we own pricing?</h3><div class=3D"qa why"><span class=3D"qa-l">W=
hy ask</span><p>Airline prices change all the time (dynamic pricing, fare c=
lasses opening and closing). If we cache search results, the price a user s=
aw may be gone by the time they book.</p></div><div class=3D"qa ans"><span =
class=3D"qa-l">Interviewer answer</span><p>No. Revenue Management (an exter=
nal system) sets prices and how many seats each fare class may sell. We sto=
re what it gives us and must never sell at a price that is no longer valid.=
</p></div></div><div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"8-trip-types-and-group-size-r=
ound-trips-connecting-flights"><span class=3D"qn">Q8</span>Trip types and g=
roup size? Round trips, connecting flights?</h3><div class=3D"qa why"><span=
 class=3D"qa-l">Why ask</span><p>Round trips and connections mean one booki=
ng touches several flights that must all succeed together or none, and sear=
ch has to build connecting itineraries. Keeping to one nonstop flight makes=
 every booking one simple database transaction.</p></div><div class=3D"qa a=
ns"><span class=3D"qa-l">Interviewer answer</span><p>Keep it simple: one-wa=
y, a single origin and destination, nonstop flights only (no connections, n=
o round trips). So <strong>every booking is exactly one flight</strong>. Up=
 to 9 passengers per booking.</p></div></div><div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"=
9-what-about-payments-refunds-and-the-24-hour-rule"><span class=3D"qn">Q9</=
span>What about payments, refunds and the 24-hour rule?</h3><div class=3D"q=
a why"><span class=3D"qa-l">Why ask</span><p>Money must never be taken twic=
e, and never taken without a seat. In the US, the DOT 24-hour rule means a =
booking made 7+ days before departure can be cancelled within 24 hours for =
a full refund (or held 24 hours without payment). <span class=3D"pill our">=
our addition</span></p></div><div class=3D"qa ans"><span class=3D"qa-l">Int=
erviewer answer</span><p>Use an external payment gateway. Support cancellat=
ion with refund or travel credit depending on fare rules, including free ca=
ncellation within 24 hours.</p></div></div><div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"10=
-do-we-handle-flight-cancellations-and-delays-irregular-operations"><span c=
lass=3D"qn">Q10</span>Do we handle flight cancellations and delays (irregul=
ar operations)?</h3><div class=3D"qa why"><span class=3D"qa-l">Why ask</spa=
n><p>When a flight is cancelled, every passenger on it must be rebooked and=
 told, sometimes thousands at once during a storm. That's a big async workl=
oad that must not slow down normal bookings. <span class=3D"pill our">our a=
ddition</span></p></div><div class=3D"qa ans"><span class=3D"qa-l">Intervie=
wer answer</span><p>Yes, at a high level: when Operations cancels a flight,=
 rebook affected passengers automatically and notify them.</p></div></div><=
/div></section><section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"-functional-requirements"><=
span class=3D"sec-n">2.</span> Functional Requirements</h2><div class=3D"ca=
rds grid2"><p class=3D"lead">Core capabilities the booking system must prov=
ide.</p><div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"1-flight-search"><span class=3D"qn">1=
</span>Flight Search</h3><p>Users search by origin, destination, date, pass=
enger count and cabin. One-way, nonstop flights only: results list that day=
's flights on the route with prices per fare, sorted by price or departure =
time.</p></div><div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"2-fare--seat-availability"><sp=
an class=3D"qn">2</span>Fare &amp; Seat Availability</h3><p>Show which fare=
s are still for sale on each flight and, for the chosen flight, a seat map =
with available seats, seat types (window, aisle, exit row) and seat prices.=
</p></div><div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"3-hold-seats-during-checkout"><span=
 class=3D"qn">3</span>Hold Seats During Checkout</h3><p>When a user starts =
a booking, temporarily hold their fare-class places and chosen seats for 15=
 minutes, so nobody else can take them while they pay. Unpaid holds expire =
and are released automatically.</p></div><div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"4-bo=
oking-payment--ticketing"><span class=3D"qn">4</span>Booking, Payment &amp;=
 Ticketing</h3><p>Create a booking (PNR with a 6-character confirmation cod=
e), take payment through a payment gateway, and issue one e-ticket per pass=
enger. One booking is one flight, and it is all-or-nothing across every pas=
senger.</p></div><div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"5-manage-booking"><span clas=
s=3D"qn">5</span>Manage Booking</h3><p>Look up a booking by confirmation co=
de and last name, and cancel with a refund or travel credit according to fa=
re rules (including free cancellation within 24 hours).</p></div><div class=
=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"6-notifications"><span class=3D"qn">6</span>Notificatio=
ns</h3><p>Send booking confirmation, e-tickets, reminders and flight-change=
 alerts through the Notification Service (our earlier design).</p></div><di=
v class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"7-disruption-handling"><span class=3D"qn">7</spa=
n>Disruption Handling</h3><p>When a flight is cancelled or significantly de=
layed, automatically rebook affected passengers and notify them.</p></div><=
/div></section><section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"-non-functional-requirement=
s"><span class=3D"sec-n">3.</span> Non-Functional Requirements</h2><div cla=
ss=3D"cards grid2"><p class=3D"lead">Quality attributes, performance criter=
ia, and availability SLAs.</p><div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"1-strong-consis=
tency-for-inventory-and-money"><span class=3D"qn">1</span>Strong Consistenc=
y for Inventory and Money</h3><p>A seat is never given to two passengers, a=
 fare class never sells above its limit, a cabin never sells more places th=
an its physical seats (no overbooking), and a customer is never charged twi=
ce or charged without getting a seat.</p></div><div class=3D"card"><h3 id=
=3D"2-high-availability"><span class=3D"qn">2</span>High Availability</h3><=
p>99.99% for search and booking. Search may degrade (slightly stale or fewe=
r results) but must stay up; booking must stay correct even if that means r=
ejecting some requests.</p></div><div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"3-low-latenc=
y"><span class=3D"qn">3</span>Low Latency</h3><p>Search under 1 second at p=
99 (mostly from cache). Seat hold under 300 ms. Payment and ticketing under=
 3=E2=80=935 seconds (bounded by the gateway).</p></div><div class=3D"card"=
><h3 id=3D"4-scalability"><span class=3D"qn">4</span>Scalability</h3><p>Rea=
d-heavy (about 1,000 searches per booking) with 10=C3=97 spikes during fare=
 sales. Writes are small in volume but concentrate on a few hot flights.</p=
></div><div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"5-durability--auditability"><span clas=
s=3D"qn">5</span>Durability &amp; Auditability</h3><p>Every booking, seat a=
nd payment change is stored and logged (who, when, why). Needed for custome=
r support, refunds, chargebacks and financial reconciliation.</p></div></di=
v></section><section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"-would-you-like-me-to-cover-ba=
ck-of-the-envelope-calculations-as-well-or-focus-on-the-high-level-design">=
<span class=3D"sec-n">4.</span> Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation</h2><p clas=
s=3D"ask">=E2=80=9CWould you like me to cover back-of-the-envelope calculat=
ions as well, or focus on the high-level design?=E2=80=9D</p><details open>=
<summary>Back-of-the-envelope (if asked)</summary><h4>Traffic</h4><div clas=
